All,

Following are the terms we should use to describe and refer to the various =
Philmac fitting product lines.  Presented are "long form" and "short form" =
term alternatives.

Customers may use different terminology and themselves be confused and in p=
articular with respect to the type of plastic pipe they are using.   It is =
necessary to clarify the customer's terminology with the customer and confi=
rm his request in specific terms with respect to the type of plastic pipe h=
e intends to use the fittings for.  Of course if the customer is submitting=
 part numbers clarification is not required unless THE DESCRIPTION THE CUST=
OMER USES IS INCONSISTENT WITH THE PART NUMBER HE USES.

Special Notes:  The use of the term "3G" to refer to the IPS-OD Philmac fit=
tings is incorrect and will lead to errors because all versions except UTC =
use the 3G "platform".

Use of the term "3G" is unnecessary for identification of the specific Phil=
mac product to be used and should be avoided.

The circumstances where the "3G" and "2G" distinctions are in play are trea=
ted by separate Blurt.

Long Form Terminology
Short Form Terminology
Philmac compression fittings for IPS-OD polyethylene pipe
IPS-OD Philmac


Philmac compression fittings for IPS-ID polyethylene pipe
IPS-ID Philmac
SIDR Philmac

Philmac compression fittings for CTS polyethylene pipe
CTS Philmac
Copper Tube Size Philmac

Philmac compression fittings for Kitec/XPA/QLine polyethylene pipe
Kitec Philmac
XPA Philmac
QLine Philmac
Philmac UTC compression fittings
Philmac UTC
UTC


Notes:

1.       The customer may be unclear about which pipe he has often just kno=
wing he has HDPE pipe, or PE pipe, or PEX, or "poly", or "poly pipe" or oth=
ers.  The term "poly" is a nick name also used for polybutylene pipe which =
these fittings will not work on.   PEX refers to a special kind of polyethy=
lene pipe on which CTS Philmac fittings can be used.  If terms other than t=
hese are used refer them to me.

2.       If the term "poly" is used it must be clarified whether polyethyle=
ne pipe (aka HDPE, aka PE) or polybutylene pipe is the subject.  If it is p=
olybutylene advise the customer the Philmac fittings are not appropriate.

3.       If it is established polyethylene pipe is the subject, clarify the=
 specific pipe type (IPS-OD, IPS-ID, CTS, Kitec/XPA/QLine).  These are not =
necessarily universally understood terms so a "back and forth" may be requi=
red.

4.       IPS-OD means the pipe is made to IPS outside diameters (OD) and th=
e extrusion of the pipe is OD controlled.  Regardless of the Standard Dimen=
sion Ratio (SDR) the OD is the same and is the IPS diameter industry conven=
tion.  This pipe is made to ASTM or AWWA standards listed on the price shee=
t.

5.       IPS-ID means the pipe is made "nominally" to IPS diameters but the=
 extrusion of the pipe is ID controlled.  This means the pipe OD varies dep=
ending on SIDR (Standard Internal Dimension Ratio).  Since the OD varies th=
e IPS-OD fittings cannot be used.  IPS-ID Philmac can only be used on the S=
IDR's listed on the SIDR price sheet.  There are other SIDR's used that the=
 standard IPS-ID Philmac fittings will not work on.  This pipe is made to A=
STM or AWWA standards listed on the price sheet.

6.       A common customer "mistake" is to refer to IPS-ID/SIDR pipe as IPS=
 pipe.  The default interpretation when using the term "IPS" is to translat=
e it as IPS-OD pipe.  Customers that only use IPS-ID pipe may not be aware =
of the finer points.  When customers ask for IPS it is safest to ask for cl=
arification between IPS-OD and IPS-ID.

7.       The acronym "SIDR" is used at times to refer to the pipe and might=
 be used to refer to the appropriate Philmac fittings.

8.       CTS refers to the Copper Tube Size OD standard.  A different PE pi=
pe made to different industry standards listed on the price sheet are made =
to the CTS OD dimensions.

9.       Kitec/XPA/QLine are three brand names of a special type of pipe fo=
r which we have the ability to supply Philmac fittings.

10.   The most precise way for a communication on the matter of which pipe =
is used would be for the customer to identify the ASTM or AWWA standard to =
which the pipe is made.  Whichever price sheet lists this standard is the P=
hilmac product line needed.  Sometimes customers don't know what the standa=
rd is.  They should be able to consult their pipe source or read the "print=
 line" on the pipe itself.

11.   The Universal Transition Coupling (UTC) fittings can be used on many =
types of pipes.  See separate documentation for further explanation.

Famous quote:  "Everything should be made as simple as possible, but not si=
mpler."

