1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">1.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>The customer may be unclear about which pipe he has=
 often just knowing he has HDPE pipe, or PE pipe, or PEX, or &#8220;poly&#8=
221;, or &#8220;poly pipe&#8221; or others.&nbsp; The term &#8220;poly&#822=
1; is a nick name also used for polybutylene pipe which these fittings will
 not work on. &nbsp;&nbsp;PEX refers to a special kind of polyethylene pipe=
 on which CTS Philmac fittings can be used.&nbsp; If terms other than these=
 are used refer them to me.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">2.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>If the term &#8220;poly&#8221; is used it must be c=
larified whether polyethylene pipe (aka HDPE, aka PE) or polybutylene pipe =
is the subject.&nbsp; If it is polybutylene advise the customer the Philmac=
 fittings are not appropriate.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">3.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>If it is established polyethylene pipe is the subje=
ct, clarify the specific pipe type (IPS-OD, IPS-ID, CTS, Kitec/XPA/QLine).&=
nbsp;
<i>These are not necessarily universally understood terms so a &#8220;back =
and forth&#8221; may be required.</i><o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">4.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>IPS-OD means the pipe is made to IPS outside diamet=
ers (OD) and the extrusion of the pipe is OD controlled.&nbsp; Regardless o=
f the Standard Dimension Ratio (SDR) the OD is the same and is the IPS diam=
eter industry convention.&nbsp; This pipe
 is made to ASTM or AWWA standards listed on the price sheet.<o:p></o:p></p=
>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">5.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>IPS-ID means the pipe is made &#8220;nominally&#822=
1; to IPS diameters but the extrusion of the pipe is ID controlled.&nbsp; T=
his means the pipe OD varies depending on SIDR (Standard Internal Dimension=
 Ratio).&nbsp; Since the OD varies the IPS-OD fittings
 cannot be used.&nbsp; IPS-ID Philmac can only be used on the SIDR&#8217;s =
listed on the SIDR price sheet.&nbsp; There are other SIDR&#8217;s used tha=
t the standard IPS-ID Philmac fittings will not work on.&nbsp; This pipe is=
 made to ASTM or AWWA standards listed on the price sheet.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">6.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>A common customer &#8220;mistake&#8221; is to refer=
 to IPS-ID/SIDR pipe as IPS pipe.&nbsp; The default interpretation when usi=
ng the term &#8220;IPS&#8221; is to translate it as IPS-OD pipe.&nbsp; Cust=
omers that only use IPS-ID pipe may not be aware of the finer points.&nbsp;
 When customers ask for IPS it is safest to ask for clarification between I=
PS-OD and IPS-ID.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">7.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>The acronym &#8220;SIDR&#8221; is used at times to =
refer to the pipe and might be used to refer to the appropriate Philmac fit=
tings.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">8.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>CTS refers to the Copper Tube Size OD standard.&nbs=
p; A different PE pipe made to different industry standards listed on the p=
rice sheet are made to the CTS OD dimensions.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">9.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&=
nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>Kitec/XPA/QLine are three brand names of a special =
type of pipe for which we have the ability to supply Philmac fittings.<o:p>=
</o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">10.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>The most precise way for a communication on the mat=
ter of which pipe is used would be for the customer to identify the ASTM or=
 AWWA standard to which the pipe is made.&nbsp; Whichever price sheet lists=
 this standard is the Philmac product
 line needed.&nbsp; Sometimes customers don&#8217;t know what the standard =
is.&nbsp; They should be able to consult their pipe source or read the &#82=
20;print line&#8221; on the pipe itself.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoListParagraph" style=3D"text-indent:-.25in;mso-list:l1 level=
1 lfo3"><![if !supportLists]><span style=3D"mso-list:Ignore">11.<span style=
=3D"font:7.0pt &quot;Times New Roman&quot;">&nbsp;&nbsp;
</span></span><![endif]>The Universal Transition Coupling (UTC) fittings ca=
n be used on many types of pipes.&nbsp; See separate documentation for furt=
her explanation.<o:p></o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal"><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>
<p class=3D"MsoNormal">Famous quote:&nbsp; &#8220;Everything should be made=
 as simple as possible, but not simpler.&#8221;<o:p></o:p></p>
</div>
